= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $m$ be the number of <facets> of the <convex polytope> $P$. Its facet description can be written
$$
P=\bigcap_{j=1}^m\{x:u_j\cdot x\leq h_j\},\qquad\lVert u_j\rVert=1.
$$
Because $r^{-1}B^n\subset P$, each supporting <closed half-space> has $h_j\geq1/r$. For any $\theta\in S^{n-1}$, follow the ray $t\theta$ to its last point in $P$. Its radius $t_\theta$ is at most one because $P\subset B^n$. At least one <facet> is active there, giving
$$
t_\theta u_j\cdot\theta=h_j\geq1/r,\qquad u_j\cdot\theta\geq1/r.
$$
Thus the <unit sphere> is covered by the $m$ <spherical caps> centred at $u_j$ with angular radius $\rho=\arccos(1/r)$. Since $r\geq\sqrt2$, this radius lies in $[\pi/4,\pi/2)$ and the <spherical cap area upper bound> applies. Subadditivity of <surface area> gives
$$
1\leq m\sin^n\rho=m(1-r^{-2})^{n/2}.
$$
Using $\log(1-x)\leq-x$ for $0<x<1$,
$$
\boxed{m\geq(1-r^{-2})^{-n/2}\geq\exp\!\left(\frac{n}{2r^2}\right).}
$$
**The <facet lower bound for ball approximations> is exponential in the dimension.**

